<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced Policy
<br class="ltx_break">Optimization for Red-Teaming Attacks</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ruohao Guo
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Work done during internship at Oracle. Correspondence to &lt;<a href="mailto:rguo48@gatech.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">rguo48@gatech.edu</a>&gt;.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Georgia Institute of Technology
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Afshin Oroojlooy
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Oracle AI
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Roshan Sridhar
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Oracle AI
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Miguel Ballesteros
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Oracle AI
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alan Ritter
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Georgia Institute of Technology
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dan Roth
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Oracle AI
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite recent rapid progress in AI safety, current large language models remain vulnerable to adversarial attacks in multi-turn interaction settings, where attackers strategically adapt their prompts across conversation turns and pose a more critical yet realistic challenge. Existing approaches that discover safety vulnerabilities either rely on manual red-teaming with human experts or employ automated methods using pre-defined templates and human-curated attack data, with most focusing on single-turn attacks. However, these methods did not explore the vast space of possible multi-turn attacks, failing to consider novel attack trajectories that emerge from complex dialogue dynamics and strategic conversation planning. This gap is particularly critical given recent findings that LLMs exhibit significantly higher vulnerability to multi-turn attacks compared to single-turn attacks. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span>, an on-policy reinforcement learning framework integrated with tree search that autonomously discovers diverse multi-turn attack strategies by treating the dialogue as a sequential decision-making problem, enabling systematic exploration without manually curated data. Through extensive experiments, our approach not only achieves more than 25.9% higher ASR across 10 target models compared to previous state-of-the-art approaches, but also effectively uncovers new attack strategies by learning optimal dialogue policies that maximize attack success across multiple turns.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Disclaimer: This paper contains potentially offensive and harmful text.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite recent advances in large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, their potential to produce harmful content when deliberately manipulated remains a significant concern.
Red-teaming, the process of adversarially testing LLMs to uncover safety vulnerabilities, is a critical step in ensuring their responsible use.
Early red-teaming efforts focused on <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single-turn attacks</span> to elicit harmful responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. However, real-world interactions with LLMs are inherently conversational, where attackers can iteratively adapt their jailbreaking strategies based on the target model’s responses. Recent studies demonstrate that <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multi-turn attacks</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahman et al., 2025</a>)</cite> achieve higher success rates than single-turn methods, as they can gradually erode safety boundaries through dialogue progression, exploit contextual dependencies across turns, and adjust tactics when initial attempts fail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While being more effective, existing multi-turn methods often rely on manually crafted heuristics or templates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and still lack a mechanism for learning long-horizon strategies. This is a crucial limitation, as the multi-turn attack should not be merely excessive trials, but a strategic planning where each turn builds toward a long-term goal. We address this gap by formulating red-teaming as a <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strategic reasoning problem in goal-oriented dialogues</span>, where an attacker agent strategically explores the dialogue space, reasons about the target model’s responses, and adaptively plans a sequence of actions to achieve a final jailbreak goal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement Learning (RL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaelbling et al., 1996</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite> offers a powerful paradigm for this problem, but applying RL to multi-turn red-teaming presents several unique challenges. <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First,</span> the exploration complexity in multi-turn dialogues is extensive, as each turn offers numerous possible responses and attack strategies, making the action space grow exponentially and difficult to explore. <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second,</span> unlike tasks like math or coding that have verifiable rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, the jailbreaking task operates with <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-verifiable rewards</span>. The outcomes are assessed by imperfect proxy models, i.e., safety guardrails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Third,</span> the policy optimization for multi-turn red-teaming might suffer from training instability due to the complex gradient updates. To address these challenges, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span>, a new on-policy RL framework designed for multi-turn strategic red-teaming. Our approach integrates three key innovations: (1) <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dialogue tree rollout with pruning</span>, which enables structured exploration on diverse attack strategies while eliminating low-quality trajectories to improve training efficiency (§<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Dialogue Tree Rollout with Pruning ‣ 3 DialTree-RPO: Dialogue Reinforced Policy Optimization with Tree Search for Strategic Red-Teaming ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>); (2) a specialized <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reward function</span> to guide policy learning in multi-turn red-teaming (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Optimization with Adaptive Masking ‣ 3 DialTree-RPO: Dialogue Reinforced Policy Optimization with Tree Search for Strategic Red-Teaming ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>); and (3) an <span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">adaptive masking</span> technique that stabilizes and improves multi-turn policy optimization (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Optimization with Adaptive Masking ‣ 3 DialTree-RPO: Dialogue Reinforced Policy Optimization with Tree Search for Strategic Red-Teaming ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Through extensive experiments across 10 target LLMs, we show that <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span> achieves an average attack success rate (ASR) of 85.3%, substantially outperforming prior approaches by 25.9% ASR. We also conduct thorough analyses, showing that our <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span> can achieve the highest ASR with the best efficiency, transfer from a small model to much larger models, and uncover new attack strategies. Our key contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize red-teaming as a conversational strategic reasoning problem and introduce <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span>, a new tree-based RL framework to discover adaptive attack strategies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span> equips with dialogue tree rollout with quality-aware pruning and adaptive masking to effectively addresses the core challenges of multi-turn RL for red-teaming.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our extensive experiments demonstrate that <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span> establishes the new state-of-the-art red-teaming method with remarkable efficiency and transferability.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Red-Teaming Attacks as Conversational Strategic Reasoning</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Problem Setup.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate the task of multi-turn red-teaming as a goal-oriented, strategic conversation between two agents: an <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D73027;">attacker</span> model <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> designed to probe the safety vulnerabilities of a <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#3C78D8;">target</span> model <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{tgt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{tgt}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of red-teaming <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FC8D59;">goals</span>, where each goal <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies unsafe content that the attacker needs to elicit from the target (e.g., “<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Give detailed instructions on how to make a bomb</span>”).
We define the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">state</span> at turn <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}=\big\{g,[(c_{1},q_{1},r_{1}),\ldots,(c_{t},q_{t},r_{t})]\big\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}=\big\{g,[(c_{1},q_{1},r_{1}),\ldots,(c_{t},q_{t},r_{t})]\big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which consists of the goal <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and the complete dialogue history up to turn <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The dialogue history at <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th turn consists of a chain-of-thought (<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D73027;">CoT</span>) reasoning step <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that strategically plans how to approach the goal, an <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D73027;">attack query</span> <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#3C78D8;">response</span> <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from the target. Given a goal <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, the attacker begins by generating an initial <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">action</span> that consists of a CoT and attack query, i.e, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(c_{1},q_{1})\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{1},q_{1})\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, based on the initial state <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}=(g,\emptyset)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}=(g,\emptyset)</annotation></semantics></math>. The target responds with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{1}\sim\pi_{\text{tgt}}(\cdot\mid q_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\sim\pi_{\text{tgt}}(\cdot\mid q_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to a new state <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}=(g,[(c_{1},q_{1},r_{1})])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}=(g,[(c_{1},q_{1},r_{1})])</annotation></semantics></math>. Subsequently, the attacker generates an action <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(c_{t},q_{t})\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid s_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{t},q_{t})\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid s_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> at each turn <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that while the attacker has <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">full</span> observability of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the target operates under <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">partial</span> observability, i.e., it cannot access the goal <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and the CoT reasoning <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c_{i}\}_{i=1}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c_{i}\}_{i=1}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the state. Given the attack query <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the previous history, the target responds with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{t}\sim\pi_{\text{tgt}}(\cdot\mid q_{t},s_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}\sim\pi_{\text{tgt}}(\cdot\mid q_{t},s_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The interaction continues until either the target is successfully jailbroken or the maximum turn limit <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is reached.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Paradigm.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our objective is to learn an attacker policy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> that elicits goal-relevant, harmful responses from the target through adaptive interaction within <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> turns. We train <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> in two stages following RL with cold start paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1 (Cold-Start SFT)</span>:
We initialize <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on red-teaming conversations paired with CoTs, in order to (i) relax the model’s safety constraints, and (ii) teach the required output format, i.e., (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) at each turn <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2 (RL with <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span>)</span>: We optimize <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with our RL algorithm (§<a href="#S3" title="3 DialTree-RPO: Dialogue Reinforced Policy Optimization with Tree Search for Strategic Red-Teaming ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) to improve the attacker’s capability to explore and exploit the target model’s vulnerabilities through multi-turn interactions. We formulate the multi-turn red-teaming RL training objective as:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{g\sim\mathcal{D},y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid g;\pi_{\text{tgt}})}\left[r_{\phi}(g,y)\right]-\beta\,\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}\left[\pi_{\theta}(y\mid g;\pi_{\text{tgt}})\,\|\,\pi_{\text{ref}}(y\mid g;\pi_{\text{tgt}})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{g\sim\mathcal{D},y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid g;\pi_{\text{tgt}})}\left[r_{\phi}(g,y)\right]-\beta\,\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}\left[\pi_{\theta}(y\mid g;\pi_{\text{tgt}})\,\|\,\pi_{\text{ref}}(y\mid g;\pi_{\text{tgt}})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the initial attack goal sampled from distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the attacker’s generated output interleaved with the target’s response, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a reference policy, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward function, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a regularization term that penalizes deviation from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby preventing <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> from drifting too far during optimization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.02286v1/tree_dialog_v6_wCoT.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:510/256;" width="510" height="256" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
<span id="S2.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Illustration of dialogue tree expansion with pruning.</span> <span id="S2.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> Each node <math id="S2.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t,k}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a state defined by the goal and dialogue histories at the <math id="S2.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th action branch at turn <math id="S2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Starting from <math id="S2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the attacker explores multiple conversation paths across turns, until the target is jailbroken or the maximum turn limit <math id="S2.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is reached. Malformed or off-topic branches are pruned at each turn. <span id="S2.F1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> At each state, the attacker generates <math id="S2.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> candidate actions consisting of a CoT and query. Each query is sent to the target to elicit a response, resulting in a new state. <span id="S2.F1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c)</span> We collect the trajectories that are not pruned from the rollout tree and assign rewards to each trajectory based on whether the target model is jailbroken or not. We set the branching factor <math id="S2.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}=3</annotation></semantics></math> for this figure.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span>: Dialogue Reinforced Policy Optimization with Tree Search for Strategic Red-Teaming</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span>, a reinforcement learning (RL) framework for multi-turn red-teaming via strategic and adaptive dialogue. <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span> consists of a tree-based rollout mechanism with pruning to efficiently explore the vast space of possible attack trajectories (§<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Dialogue Tree Rollout with Pruning ‣ 3 DialTree-RPO: Dialogue Reinforced Policy Optimization with Tree Search for Strategic Red-Teaming ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), a reward function for red-teaming scenarios where we detect the outcomes of jailbreaking through a specialized safety guardrail (§<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" title="Reward Design. ‣ 3.2 Optimization with Adaptive Masking ‣ 3 DialTree-RPO: Dialogue Reinforced Policy Optimization with Tree Search for Strategic Red-Teaming ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), and an adaptive masking technique that improves training stability and performance for multi-turn RL (§<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" title="Adaptive Masking. ‣ 3.2 Optimization with Adaptive Masking ‣ 3 DialTree-RPO: Dialogue Reinforced Policy Optimization with Tree Search for Strategic Red-Teaming ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
We present the details of <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix E Algorithm Outline of DialTree-RPO ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Dialogue Tree Rollout with Pruning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To train the attacker policy <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, a fundamental challenge lies in how we can systematically explore the exponentially large space of possible attack conversations to identify the target’s vulnerabilities. Standard GRPO approaches typically sample independent trajectories, which in our case correspond to distinct dialogues between two agents. Such linear rollouts cannot explore candidate attacker actions to learn from controlled comparisons at each turn.
To address this limitation, we introduce <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dialogue tree rollout</span>, which enables structured exploration to discover diverse and novel attack strategies within a constrained search space anchored by the shared dialogue context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Training Paradigm. ‣ 2 Red-Teaming Attacks as Conversational Strategic Reasoning ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the dialogue tree rollout with pruning.
Starting from an initial goal <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, the tree expands while the attacker iteratively interacts with the target across multiple branches.
At the beginning of turn <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, for each active state denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> from the previous turn, the attacker samples <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> distinct actions <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{(c_{t,k},q_{t,k})\}_{k=1}^{n}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid s_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(c_{t,k},q_{t,k})\}_{k=1}^{n}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid s_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Each generated attack query <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t,k}</annotation></semantics></math>, along with the dialogue history in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (without the goal and CoTs), is sent to the target model to elicit a response, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{t,k}\sim\pi_{\text{tgt}}(\cdot\mid q_{t,k},s_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t,k}\sim\pi_{\text{tgt}}(\cdot\mid q_{t,k},s_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, which yields a new state <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t,k}</annotation></semantics></math> with the triplet <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{t,k},q_{t,k},r_{t,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{t,k},q_{t,k},r_{t,k})</annotation></semantics></math> incorporated. Each new state will first be evaluated by a safety guardrail to determine whether the target model is jailbroken, i.e., eliciting a harmful response or not. If not, we will assess whether the state should be pruned according to our designed criteria. Among the new states <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{t,k}\}_{k=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{t,k}\}_{k=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> at turn <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the states that are not jailbroken or pruned will remain active for subsequent turns. All expansions will be stopped when the maximum turn limit <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is reached.
After the tree rollout completes, we collect dialogue trajectories by traversing all paths from the root node <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the leaf nodes, excluding the branches that were pruned during expansion. Each collected trajectory <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a complete attack dialogue and is assigned a scalar reward <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> based on whether it successfully jailbroke the target (detailed in §<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" title="Reward Design. ‣ 3.2 Optimization with Adaptive Masking ‣ 3 DialTree-RPO: Dialogue Reinforced Policy Optimization with Tree Search for Strategic Red-Teaming ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), which we use to compute group-relative advantages for policy optimization.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pruning Criteria.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure the effectiveness and efficiency of tree search, we employ three pruning criteria to prune low-quality branches at each turn. <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First</span>, we enforce <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">format validity</em> by discarding nodes where the attacker produces malformed outputs (i.e., missing either the CoT or the query), as these branches cannot continue the dialogue with the desired format. <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second</span>, we maintain <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">topic adherence</em> by pruning nodes where the conversation has drifted away from the original goal as determined by an on-topic classifier, ensuring the attacker learns to pursue coherent and goal-directed strategies. <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Third</span>, to prevent exponential growth in branches, we apply <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">branch limiting</em> by retaining at most <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> nodes per turn through random subsampling from those that pass both format and topic filters. Additional implementation details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Reinforcement Learning ‣ Appendix C Implementation Details ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Optimization with Adaptive Masking</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward Design.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reward function provides the primary training signal for RL optimization. We design a reward for the attacker that encourages generating dialogues that successfully elicit goal-relevant unsafe responses from the target. Specifically, we use HarmAug-Guard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>), a lightweight yet competitive guardrail classifier, to compute dialogue-level harmfulness scores (see Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Reward Model Selection ‣ Appendix D Additional Experiments ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> for reward model experiments).
Given a dialogue trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(c_{1},q_{1},r_{1},\dots,c_{T},q_{T},r_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(c_{1},q_{1},r_{1},\dots,c_{T},q_{T},r_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> interaction turns and a goal <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, the reward is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R=\begin{cases}1,\,\,\,\,\text{if $\exists\,t\leq T:\;r_{\phi}(g;q_{t},r_{t})&gt;\eta$}\\
0,\,\,\,\,\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.847em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∃</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.847em">,</mo><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\begin{cases}1,\,\,\,\,\text{if $\exists\,t\leq T:\;r_{\phi}(g;q_{t},r_{t})&gt;\eta$}\\
0,\,\,\,\,\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a decision threshold on harmfulness. We set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> by default.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dialogue GRPO.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid introducing an additional value function, we adopt Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> in our multi-turn dialogue setting to train the attacker policy <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each goal <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample a group of trajectories <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tau^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tau^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> from the old attacker policy <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a frozen target model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{tgt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{tgt}}</annotation></semantics></math> via dialogue tree rollout, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the group size. We optimize the policy by maximizing the following objective:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{J}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{g\sim\mathcal{D},\{\tau^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\cdot|g;\pi_{\text{tgt}})}\Bigg[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{\left|\tau^{(i)}\right|}\sum_{t=1}^{\left|\tau^{(i)}\right|}\min\Bigg(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(\tau_{t}^{(i)}|g,\tau_{&lt;t}^{(i)};\pi_{\text{tgt}})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\tau_{t}^{(i)}|g,\tau_{&lt;t}^{(i)};\pi_{\text{tgt}})}\hat{A}_{t}^{(i)},\\
&amp;\hskip 90.00014pt\text{clip}(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(\tau_{t}^{(i)}|g,\tau_{&lt;t}^{(i)};\pi_{\text{tgt}})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\tau_{t}^{(i)}|g,\tau_{&lt;t}^{(i)};\pi_{\text{tgt}})},1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon)\hat{A}_{t}^{(i)}\Bigg)-\beta\mathbb{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\left|\right|\pi_{\text{ref}})\Bigg]\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>τ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow></msup><mi>min</mi><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>𝔻</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{J}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{g\sim\mathcal{D},\{\tau^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\cdot|g;\pi_{\text{tgt}})}\Bigg[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{\left|\tau^{(i)}\right|}\sum_{t=1}^{\left|\tau^{(i)}\right|}\min\Bigg(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(\tau_{t}^{(i)}|g,\tau_{&lt;t}^{(i)};\pi_{\text{tgt}})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\tau_{t}^{(i)}|g,\tau_{&lt;t}^{(i)};\pi_{\text{tgt}})}\hat{A}_{t}^{(i)},\\
&amp;\hskip 90.00014pt\text{clip}(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(\tau_{t}^{(i)}|g,\tau_{&lt;t}^{(i)};\pi_{\text{tgt}})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\tau_{t}^{(i)}|g,\tau_{&lt;t}^{(i)};\pi_{\text{tgt}})},1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon)\hat{A}_{t}^{(i)}\Bigg)-\beta\mathbb{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}\left|\right|\pi_{\text{ref}})\Bigg]\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are hyperparameters, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{t}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{t}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the advantage computed based on the relative rewards of trajectories inside each group, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reference policy initialized with the SFT model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive Masking.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In multi-turn red-teaming, the attacker needs to generate structured outputs containing format tokens (e.g., <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FE8CA7;">&lt;Thinking i&gt;</span> for CoT and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#C25773;">&lt;Attack question i&gt;</span> for the attack query) at each turn to ensure the dialogue trajectories are valid and meaningful. While the attacker learned to follow this format in SFT, we observe significant <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">format unlearning</em>, i.e., the format-following capability is catastrophically forgotten in the RL stage. The fraction of invalid outputs (missing either CoT or query components) grows from near-zero (after SFT) to over 70% in RL. This degradation severely reduces the efficiency and stability of tree search. This could be due to updating against negative-advantage trajectories (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 60\%</annotation></semantics></math> in early training) might also inadvertently penalize the format tokens, thereby eroding the format-following capability acquired in SFT.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this issue, we propose an adaptive masking mechanism that selectively protects format tokens from gradient updates in negative-advantage trajectories. For each dialogue trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> with group-relative advantage <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we mask the loss computation on format tokens when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing the model to penalize poor red-teaming strategies without compromising its structural formatting ability. When <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we do not apply masking to strengthen both successful attack patterns and the format structures in policy learning. This adaptive masking maintains format consistency without affecting policy learning from both positive and negative feedback. We empirically validate the effectiveness of our masking strategy in §<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Effect of Adaptive Masking ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experiment Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the SFT stage, we fine-tune the attacker model on 397 conversations along with CoTs we curated following the guidelines in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2025c)</a></cite>. For <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span> training, we randomly sample 500 jailbreaking goals from AdvBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, DangerousQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shaikh et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and CatQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhardwaj et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. For evaluation, we construct a separate test set of 200 goals from HarmBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
We ensure evaluation datasets do not share any goals with training data in order to assess the robustness and generalization of the learned attacker policy. Details of datasets are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Datasets ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation Details.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct as the base model of our attacker. During <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span> training, the attacker interacts with a frozen target model, Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct, to explore dialogue trajectories and learn effective red-teaming strategies. We set the maximum number of dialogue turns to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}=5</annotation></semantics></math>, the branching factor to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math>, and the group size to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=32</annotation></semantics></math> by default. During evaluation, to reflect realistic constraints, we limit the number of attack queries for each target model, since API providers could enforce request quotas or block clients who exceed usage or trigger safety refusals multiple times. While our method can be used with or without tree search during inference, we do not enable tree search at test time to avoid excessive interactions with target models and reduce inference cost. More implementation details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Implementation Details ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Target Models.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the attacker in multi-turn conversations (5 turns by default) against a wide range of new target models, including proprietary models such as GPT-4o, GPT-4.1-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and Gemini-2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Comanici et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, as well as open-source models such as Llama 3.1 and 3.3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Mistral-v0.3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and Gemma-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              All models evaluated in this work are instruction-tuned. For brevity, we omit “-instruct” in model names.</span></span></span> We also include recent reasoning models, OpenAI-o3-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaech et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and Grok-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">xAI, 2025</a>)</cite>, to examine whether the attacker can exploit stronger models with enhanced reasoning and safety mechanisms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DialTree-RPO</span> against several widely used baselines. GCG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> optimizes discrete token sequences (often nonsensical) to elicit affirmative answers to harmful requests. PAIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> uses an LLM to propose and refine attack prompts with the target model iteratively. TAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mehrotra et al., 2024</a>)</cite> extends PAIR by incorporating tree search and pruning to improve exploration efficiency. Jailbreak-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025d</a>)</cite> employs RL to explore single-turn attack prompts. MTSA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025c</a>)</cite> uses DPO to train an attacker on preference data from resampled multi-turn dialogues. AutoDAN<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>Turbo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> utilizes a black-box agent to build and retrieve a strategy library by reusing text modifications that improve jailbreak scores. A detailed comparison of baselines is provided in Appendix Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Reinforcement Learning with DialTree-RPO. ‣ Appendix B Datasets ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation and Metrics.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahman et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we use GPT-4o to assess response harmfulness on a 1 to 5 Likert scale. We consider attacks successful only when GPT-4o assigns the highest harmfulness score of 5. To validate GPT-4o as a reliable judge, we randomly sampled 30 dialogues (132 query-response pairs in total) and had three human annotators (authors) independently rate responses using the same 5-point scale. The Cohen’s Kappa between human and GPT-4o ratings reached a substantial agreement score of 74.7%, supporting the reliability of our automated evaluation approach.
Details of human evaluation are described in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Human Evaluation and LLM Judge Reliability ‣ Appendix D Additional Experiments ‣ Tree-based Dialogue Reinforced PolicyOptimization for Red-Teaming Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>. We use attack success rate (ASR) as evaluation metric, which is defined as the percentage of test cases that successfully elicit the targeted harmful behavior.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>
<span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main results</span> in terms of Attack Success Rate (ASR; %) on HarmBench subset.
</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_logical-block">
            
